def S(h, t, *children):
    return h, [(t, list(children))]

rows = [Q(
 'Hypertension-mediated organ damage', 'Arterial Stiffness and Isolated Systolic Hypertension',
 'A 76-year-old has confirmed BP 168/66 mmHg. There is no aortic regurgitation, anemia or thyrotoxicosis, and stroke volume is not increased. Which vascular change best explains this systolic–diastolic pattern?',
 'Reduced compliance of the aorta and other large elastic arteries', [
  ('Increased resistance of small arterioles with preserved aortic compliance', 'Increased arteriolar resistance is a major contributor to hypertension, especially a diastolic or combined pattern. It is less satisfactory than loss of conduit-artery compliance for the marked pulse-pressure widening supplied with unchanged stroke volume.'),
  ('Increased aortic compliance with prolonged elastic recoil', 'A more compliant aorta buffers ventricular ejection and supports diastolic flow. That buffering tends to limit the systolic rise rather than generate the wide pulse pressure of this older patient.'),
  ('Increased cardiac output from peripheral arteriovenous shunting', 'A large arteriovenous shunt can create a high-output state with widened pulse pressure. It is a relevant alternative mechanism when stroke volume and flow are increased, whereas the stem supplies unchanged stroke volume and an age-associated isolated systolic pattern.')],
 [S('Arterial Mechanics',
   '**Loss of aortic compliance increases the pressure generated by a given stroke volume.** Aging-related changes in elastin, collagen and arterial wall properties increase pulsatile ventricular load and commonly produce isolated systolic hypertension.',
   'Pulse pressure is systolic minus diastolic pressure: here it is 102 mmHg. A wide pulse pressure is a clue, not a specific diagnostic test; high-output states and aortic regurgitation are competing explanations excluded in this stem.',
   'Higher pulse-wave velocity and altered reflected waves can contribute to central systolic load. Their relative contributions vary; earlier reflection timing alone should not be presented as the complete explanation for arterial aging. Mean pressure, peripheral resistance and pulsatile pressure describe related but distinct hemodynamic properties.'),
  S('Clinical Consequences and Evaluation',
   '**Persistent systolic hypertension in an older adult remains a treatable cardiovascular risk.** Increased ventricular pressure load promotes hypertrophy and impaired relaxation, while a low diastolic pressure may complicate coronary perfusion and treatment tolerance.',
   'Confirm measurements and the out-of-office pattern. Assess standing BP, symptoms, renal function, albuminuria and cardiovascular disease. Echocardiography is directed by suspected structural disease, heart-failure findings or relevant ECG abnormalities rather than ordered solely to measure a pulse-pressure difference.',
   'Use an individualized guideline target and monitor standing symptoms and renal/electrolyte effects during titration. Do not dismiss a high systolic value as normal aging or chase it with rapid lowering that causes ischemic or postural symptoms. Conversely, a modest diastolic value is not by itself a reason to abandon systolic treatment. The diagnosis concerns the measured phenotype; deciding whether a particular regimen is tolerated requires follow-up.')],
 ['STIFFNESS', 'FRAMINGHAM_HEMODYNAMICS', 'ESC2024'],
 table=(['Chronic pressure-related injury', 'Useful clinical assessment'], [
  ['Concentric LV remodeling and impaired relaxation', 'ECG; echocardiography when clinically indicated'],
  ['Kidney injury', 'Creatinine/eGFR and urine ACR; establish persistence before assigning CKD'],
  ['Cerebral small-vessel disease', 'Clinical neurological/cognitive assessment; imaging for an appropriate indication'],
  ['Retinal vascular injury', 'Fundus examination, particularly in severe hypertension or visual symptoms'],
 ])),
 Q('Secondary hypertension', 'Hypercortisolism Testing During Oral Estrogen Use',
 'A 35-year-old with hypertension has progressive proximal weakness, easy bruising and wide violaceous striae. She takes an estrogen-containing oral contraceptive, uses no glucocorticoids and has a regular sleep schedule. Serum cortisol is 4.2 µg/dL after a 1-mg overnight dexamethasone test. Which next step best clarifies endogenous cortisol excess before localization?',
 'Obtain late-night salivary cortisol on two separate nights', [
  ('Obtain pituitary contrast MRI', 'Pituitary MRI becomes useful in an appropriate ACTH-dependent localization pathway after endogenous hypercortisolism is established. Incidental pituitary lesions are common enough that imaging before biochemical confirmation can misdirect the diagnosis.'),
  ('Obtain adrenal CT with washout characterization', 'Adrenal imaging is used when an adrenal source is supported, typically with confirmed cortisol excess and suppressed ACTH. A single potentially confounded serum suppression result does not establish either cortisol excess or its adrenal origin.'),
  ('Perform an 8-mg overnight dexamethasone test', 'High-dose dexamethasone has sometimes been used as an adjunct in differentiating ACTH-dependent sources, with important limitations. It is not the preferred confirmation test for an oral-estrogen-confounded low-dose screen and does not replace reliable initial biochemical assessment.')],
 [S('Cortisol Binding and Test Interpretation',
   '**Oral estrogen raises cortisol-binding globulin and can elevate total serum cortisol despite adequate suppression of free cortisol.** The abnormal low-dose result therefore warrants confirmation rather than immediate pituitary or adrenal localization.',
   'The overnight test commonly gives 1 mg dexamethasone near 23:00 and measures serum cortisol around 08:00. A result above 1.8 µg/dL is an abnormal screen under the usual sensitive cutoff, not proof of a particular tumor.',
   'Late-night saliva assesses free cortisol and loss of the circadian nadir without the same binding-globulin effect. Obtain at least two samples and interpret against the laboratory’s assay-specific range. A regular sleep schedule makes this option suitable; shift work or disrupted sleep can undermine interpretation.'),
  S('Confirmation and Etiological Evaluation',
   '**Choose complementary biochemical testing and review confounders before assigning a source.** Repeated urinary free cortisol collections are another first-line option, while exogenous glucocorticoid exposure must be sought explicitly.',
   'Review adherence to dexamethasone, interacting drugs that change its clearance, alcohol-related or other non-neoplastic hypercortisolism and conditions affecting cortisol binding. If a serum suppression test is repeated after stopping oral estrogen, arrange suitable contraception and an adequate washout rather than stopping it without a plan.',
   'Concordant positive results on appropriate tests support proceeding to ACTH-guided source evaluation. Discordance, cyclic symptoms or high clinical suspicion despite normal testing merits specialist reassessment. Treat the associated BP, glucose and potassium abnormalities while the investigation proceeds; biochemical clarification and cardiovascular-risk treatment are complementary. The progressive discriminatory physical findings justify assessment, whereas uncomplicated hypertension or obesity alone does not justify indiscriminate cortisol screening.')],
 ['CUSHING_DIAGNOSIS', 'CUSHING_CONSENSUS']),
 Q('Secondary hypertension', 'Renal-Artery Duplex: Anatomy, Hemodynamics and Causality',
 'In a patient being evaluated for renovascular hypertension, a technically adequate renal duplex shows a focal renal-artery peak systolic velocity of 240 cm/s and a renal–aortic velocity ratio of 4.0. Which interpretation best follows the ESC 2024 vascular guideline?',
 'The findings support significant renal-artery narrowing but do not alone establish benefit from revascularization', [
  ('The findings support severe renal parenchymal disease without renal-artery narrowing', 'Parenchymal disease can produce hypertension and abnormal intrarenal indices, but focal main-artery velocity acceleration with a raised renal–aortic ratio supports an arterial stenotic lesion. Parenchymal assessment remains complementary rather than the explanation of these specific measurements.'),
  ('The findings support a critical lesion for which catheter angiography should be the next test', 'Catheter angiography is appropriate when intervention is being considered or noninvasive testing leaves an important unresolved question. These velocities support narrowing but do not establish a critical lesion or a clinical indication for an invasive next step; stable atherosclerotic disease commonly remains on medical care.'),
  ('The findings support an arteriovenous shunt as the primary explanation for the raised ratio', 'An arteriovenous communication can increase flow but requires its own Doppler features, including an appropriate low-resistance arterial and arterialized venous pattern. Focal stenotic acceleration and the supplied ratio are interpreted in a renal-artery stenosis pathway rather than diagnosing a shunt from velocity alone.')],
 [S('Duplex Principle and Thresholds',
   '**Duplex combines anatomical imaging with flow velocity and waveform assessment.** A focal accelerated jet and a raised renal–aortic velocity ratio support stenosis when the examination is technically reliable.',
   'ESC 2024 reports renal-artery peak systolic velocity above 200 cm/s for greater than 50% stenosis and a renal–aortic ratio above 3.5 for at least 60% stenosis. The supplied values exceed both criteria; these are named guideline criteria rather than a universal laboratory calibration.',
   'The ratio divides renal-artery by aortic peak systolic velocity. Aortic flow abnormalities, Doppler angle, vessel tortuosity, accessory arteries, obesity and bowel gas can limit interpretation. Post-stenotic turbulence and tardus–parvus intrarenal waveforms may provide supporting information.'),
  S('Competing Tests and Management Implications',
   '**An anatomical or velocity threshold does not prove that the stenosis causes the patient’s hypertension.** Review onset, treatment resistance, kidney function, renal size and recurrent circulatory episodes.',
   'CTA defines vascular anatomy well but involves iodinated contrast and radiation. MRA offers another anatomical route with modality- and contrast-specific limitations. Invasive angiography is mainly reserved for unresolved diagnostic questions or planned intervention and can measure translesional gradients.',
   'Continue BP and vascular-risk treatment when stable atherosclerotic stenosis is managed medically. Recurrent flash pulmonary edema, progressive ischemic kidney dysfunction or selected refractory cases may justify specialist revascularization review. Assess kidney viability and distinguish atherosclerotic ostial disease from FMD, for which balloon angioplasty rather than routine primary stenting is the usual intervention when indicated. An equivocal or technically inadequate study requires another assessment rather than forced grading from an unreliable velocity.')],
 ['ESC_VASCULAR2024', 'RENAL_DUPLEX']),
]

rows[0]['sections'].append(S('Electrical Evidence of Hypertrophy',
 '**ECG voltage supports but cannot exclude structural LV hypertrophy.** At standard calibration, the ESC/ESH table lists Sokolow–Lyon S in V1 plus R in V5 above 35 mm, or R in aVL at least 11 mm; Cornell voltage is S in V3 plus R in aVL above 28 mm in men or 20 mm in women.',
 'Voltage depends on habitus, lead placement and conduction. Interpret the tracing with its repolarization pattern and clinical context; echocardiography is more sensitive for structural hypertrophy when knowing the geometry or function will change management.'))
rows[0]['refs'].append('ESC_ECG_LVH')
